(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("k8s-network",{"k8s-network/the-kubernetes-network-model":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes does not implement networking itself. It defines rules that any network must satisfy and delegates the work to a plugin. Knowing the rules tells you what should be possible, which is what you need when something is not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The rules every Kubernetes network must satisfy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How the kubelet, the runtime and the CNI plugin create a pod network\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pod CIDRs, node CIDRs and service CIDRs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The three kinds of traffic: pod to pod, pod to service, outside to service\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-model\">The model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The network model has a few simple rules. Every pod gets its own IP address. Pods on any node can reach pods on any other node using those addresses, without network address translation. Agents on a node, such as the kubelet, can reach all pods on that node. Containers inside one pod share a network namespace and talk over localhost. Services add a stable virtual IP on top, and are a separate layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-a-pod-gets-its-address\">How a pod gets its address\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When the kubelet starts a pod, the container runtime creates a sandbox with a network namespace. The kubelet then calls the CNI plugin, a binary configured in \u003ccode>/etc/cni/net.d\u003c/code>, with the namespace and the pod identity. The plugin creates a virtual ethernet pair, puts one end in the pod and the other on the node, assigns an IP from the node pod range through IP address management, and sets up routes. When the pod is deleted, the plugin releases the address.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"address-ranges\">Address ranges\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Three ranges must not overlap with each other or with your real networks. The pod CIDR is where pod IPs come from, and each node usually receives a slice, a /24 by default with kubeadm. The service CIDR is where ClusterIPs come from and exists only as rules on nodes. The node network carries node addresses. Overlap with an office or cloud range is a common cause of unreachable destinations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,PODCIDR:.spec.podCIDR\nkubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m1 service-cluster-ip-range\nls /etc/cni/net.d\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,PODCIDR:.spec.podCIDR\nkubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m1 service-cluster-ip-range\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /etc/cni/net.d\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-kinds-of-traffic\">Three kinds of traffic\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pod to pod traffic uses pod IPs directly and depends on the CNI. Pod to service traffic goes to a ClusterIP, which kube-proxy or an eBPF datapath rewrites to a pod IP. Traffic from outside enters through a NodePort, a load balancer or an ingress or gateway, and then reaches a service. Each kind has its own failure signature, so identify which one you are debugging first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-quick-address-check\">A quick address check\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import ipaddress\npod = ipaddress.ip_network(&quot;10.244.0.0/16&quot;)\nsvc = ipaddress.ip_network(&quot;10.96.0.0/12&quot;)\nnode = ipaddress.ip_network(&quot;10.0.0.0/16&quot;)\nprint(&quot;pod/service overlap:&quot;, pod.overlaps(svc))\nprint(&quot;pod/node overlap:&quot;, pod.overlaps(node))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> ipaddress\npod = ipaddress.ip_network(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.244.0.0/16&quot;\u003c/span>)\nsvc = ipaddress.ip_network(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.96.0.0/12&quot;\u003c/span>)\nnode = ipaddress.ip_network(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10.0.0.0/16&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pod/service overlap:&quot;\u003c/span>, pod.overlaps(svc))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pod/node overlap:&quot;\u003c/span>, pod.overlaps(node))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"kl&gt;rt;rt&gt;cni;cni&gt;ipam;cni&gt;pod\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The Kubernetes network model: pods, nodes and the CNI\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubelet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rt\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runtime\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cni\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CNI plugin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ipam\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">IPAM\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod network\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, kubelet to Runtime; 2, Runtime to CNI plugin; 3, CNI plugin to IPAM; 4, CNI plugin to Pod network.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kl\">\u003cdt>kubelet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Asks the runtime for a sandbox.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Runtime down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rt\">\u003cdt>Runtime \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates the namespace.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Sandbox error.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cni\">\u003cdt>CNI plugin \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Wires and addresses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No config file.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ipam\">\u003cdt>IPAM \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allocates the IP.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Range exhausted.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod network \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Veth, IP and routes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overlap.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-pod-range-that-overlapped-the-office-network\">Incident: A pod range that overlapped the office network\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Pods could not reach an internal database after the cluster was built.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec web-1 -- curl -s -m 3 10.244.5.20:5432\nip route get 10.244.5.20\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-1 -- curl -s -m 3 10.244.5.20:5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">curl: (28) Connection timed out after 3001 milliseconds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ip route get 10.244.5.20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">10.244.5.20 via 10.244.0.1 dev cni0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The database lived in an office network that used the same range as the pod CIDR, so the node routed traffic for it to a local bridge instead of the office gateway. Rebuilding with a non-overlapping pod range was the real fix. Choose address ranges before installing, because changing them later is painful.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing a pod CIDR that overlaps real networks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming Services have network interfaces\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing up the service CIDR and the pod CIDR\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing two CNI plugins\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that the CNI must exist before pods can start\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-model","text":"The model"},{"id":"how-a-pod-gets-its-address","text":"How a pod gets its address"},{"id":"address-ranges","text":"Address ranges"},{"id":"three-kinds-of-traffic","text":"Three kinds of traffic"},{"id":"a-quick-address-check","text":"A quick address check"},{"id":"incident-a-pod-range-that-overlapped-the-office-network","text":"Incident: A pod range that overlapped the office network"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Cluster networking","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/networking/"},{"t":"Network plugins","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/compute-storage-net/network-plugins/"}],"text":"Kubernetes does not implement networking itself. It defines rules that any network must satisfy and delegates the work to a plugin. Knowing the rules tells you what should be possible, which is what you need when something is not. What you will learn The rules every Kubernetes network must satisfy How the kubelet, the runtime and the CNI plugin create a pod network Pod CIDRs, node CIDRs and service CIDRs The three kinds of traffic: pod to pod, pod to service, outside to service The model The network model has a few simple rules. Every pod gets its own IP address. Pods on any node can reach pods on any other node using those addresses, without network address translation. Agents on a node, such as the kubelet, can reach all pods on that node. Containers inside one pod share a network namespace and talk over localhost. Services add a stable virtual IP on top, and are a separate layer. How a pod gets its address When the kubelet starts a pod, the container runtime creates a sandbox with a network namespace. The kubelet then calls the CNI plugin, a binary configured in /etc/cni/net.d , with the namespace and the pod identity. The plugin creates a virtual ethernet pair, puts one end in the pod and the other on the node, assigns an IP from the node pod range through IP address management, and sets up routes. When the pod is deleted, the plugin releases the address. Address ranges Three ranges must not overlap with each other or with your real networks. The pod CIDR is where pod IPs come from, and each node usually receives a slice, a /24 by default with kubeadm. The service CIDR is where ClusterIPs come from and exists only as rules on nodes. The node network carries node addresses. Overlap with an office or cloud range is a common cause of unreachable destinations. kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,PODCIDR:.spec.podCIDR kubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m1 service-cluster-ip-range ls /etc/cni/net.d Three kinds of traffic Pod to pod traffic uses pod IPs directly and depends on the CNI. Pod to service traffic goes to a ClusterIP, which kube-proxy or an eBPF datapath rewrites to a pod IP. Traffic from outside enters through a NodePort, a load balancer or an ingress or gateway, and then reaches a service. Each kind has its own failure signature, so identify which one you are debugging first. A quick address check import ipaddress pod = ipaddress.ip_network( \"10.244.0.0/16\" ) svc = ipaddress.ip_network( \"10.96.0.0/12\" ) node = ipaddress.ip_network( \"10.0.0.0/16\" ) print ( \"pod/service overlap:\" , pod.overlaps(svc)) print ( \"pod/node overlap:\" , pod.overlaps(node)) The Kubernetes network model: pods, nodes and the CNI Flow in order: 1, kubelet to Runtime; 2, Runtime to CNI plugin; 3, CNI plugin to IPAM; 4, CNI plugin to Pod network. Service Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. kubelet Service What it does: Asks the runtime for a sandbox. If it fails or is weak: Runtime down. Runtime Step What it does: Creates the namespace. If it fails or is weak: Sandbox error. CNI plugin Step What it does: Wires and addresses. If it fails or is weak: No config file. IPAM Data store What it does: Allocates the IP. If it fails or is weak: Range exhausted. Pod network Service What it does: Veth, IP and routes. If it fails or is weak: Overlap. Incident: A pod range that overlapped the office network Incident Pods could not reach an internal database after the cluster was built. $ kubectl exec web-1 -- curl -s -m 3 10.244.5.20:5432 curl: (28) Connection timed out after 3001 milliseconds $ ip route get 10.244.5.20 10.244.5.20 via 10.244.0.1 dev cni0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The database lived in an office network that used the same range as the pod CIDR, so the node routed traffic for it to a local bridge instead of the office gateway. Rebuilding with a non-overlapping pod range was the real fix. Choose address ran"},"k8s-network/cni-plugins-overlays-routing-and-ebpf":{"html":"\u003cp>Several CNI plugins satisfy the same network model in different ways. They differ in performance, in the features they add and in how you debug them. A choice made at cluster creation is hard to undo, so it is worth understanding the families.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Overlay and encapsulation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Native routing with BGP or cloud routes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>eBPF dataplanes and what they replace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policy support and observability features\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to choose\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"overlay-networks\">Overlay networks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An overlay wraps pod packets inside packets between nodes, commonly with VXLAN or Geneve. It works over almost any underlying network, because the nodes only need to reach each other. The cost is a small overhead per packet, a lower effective MTU and an extra layer to debug. Flannel with VXLAN is a simple example.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-routing\">Native routing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A routed design gives pods addresses that the underlying network can route. Nodes advertise their pod ranges with BGP, as in Calico or Cilium in routing mode, or the cloud provider programs routes. There is no encapsulation, so performance is closer to the host network, and tools such as traceroute show the real path. It needs a network that cooperates, or cloud integration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ebpf-dataplanes\">eBPF dataplanes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>eBPF lets a plugin attach small verified programs to kernel hooks. Cilium uses it to implement forwarding, service load balancing and policy without long iptables chains, and can replace kube-proxy. Benefits are scale and visibility, with identity based policy and flow observability. The costs are kernel version requirements and a new way to debug.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"features-to-compare\">Features to compare\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compare support for NetworkPolicy and any extensions, encryption between nodes, observability of flows, IPv6 and dual stack, multi-cluster connectivity, maximum cluster size and operational maturity. Check that the plugin supports your environment, for example a managed cloud with its own CNI.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -E &quot;calico|cilium|flannel|weave&quot;\nip -d link show | grep -E &quot;vxlan|geneve&quot; | head -3\nip route | head\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;calico|cilium|flannel|weave&quot;\u003c/span>\nip -d \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">link\u003c/span> show | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;vxlan|geneve&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\nip route | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with requirements: network policy, scale, encryption, the base platform. A small lab can use a simple overlay. A production cluster usually wants policy support and good observability, which points to Calico or Cilium. Run the choice through a test in your environment before you commit, and plan how you will upgrade the plugin, since it runs on every node.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ov&gt;rt;rt&gt;ebpf;ebpf&gt;pol;pol&gt;obs\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">CNI plugins: overlays, routing and eBPF\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ov\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Overlay\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rt\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Native routing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ebpf\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">eBPF dataplane\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obs\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Overlay to Native routing; 2, Native routing to eBPF dataplane; 3, eBPF dataplane to Policy; 4, Policy to Observability.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ov\">\u003cdt>Overlay \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> VXLAN or Geneve.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> MTU too large.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rt\">\u003cdt>Native routing \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> BGP or cloud routes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Network refuses routes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ebpf\">\u003cdt>eBPF dataplane \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Kernel programs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Old kernel.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Enforced by the plugin.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Plugin without support.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obs\">\u003cdt>Observability \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Flow visibility.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not enabled.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-large-responses-that-hung\">Incident: Large responses that hung\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Small requests worked, but any response over a certain size stalled between pods on different nodes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec web-1 -- ping -c1 -M do -s 1472 10.244.2.9\nip link show vxlan.calico | grep mtu\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-1 -- ping -c1 -M \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span> -s 1472 10.244.2.9\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ping: local error: message too long, mtu=1450\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ip \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">link\u003c/span> show vxlan.calico | grep mtu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">mtu 1450\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The overlay added headers, so the pod interface MTU was 1450, but the application tested paths with a larger size and relied on path MTU discovery that a firewall dropped. Small packets passed, and large ones stalled. Setting a consistent MTU across node and pod interfaces and allowing the relevant ICMP messages fixed it. Overlay overhead is a classic cause of &quot;works for small requests&quot; faults.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ignoring MTU with overlays\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a plugin without policy support when you need policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replacing the CNI on a running cluster casually\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running an eBPF plugin on an unsupported kernel\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not planning plugin upgrades\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"overlay-networks","text":"Overlay networks"},{"id":"native-routing","text":"Native routing"},{"id":"ebpf-dataplanes","text":"eBPF dataplanes"},{"id":"features-to-compare","text":"Features to compare"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"incident-large-responses-that-hung","text":"Incident: Large responses that hung"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Network plugins","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/compute-storage-net/network-plugins/"},{"t":"CNI specification","u":"https://github.com/containernetworking/cni/blob/main/SPEC.md"}],"text":"Several CNI plugins satisfy the same network model in different ways. They differ in performance, in the features they add and in how you debug them. A choice made at cluster creation is hard to undo, so it is worth understanding the families. What you will learn Overlay and encapsulation Native routing with BGP or cloud routes eBPF dataplanes and what they replace Policy support and observability features How to choose Overlay networks An overlay wraps pod packets inside packets between nodes, commonly with VXLAN or Geneve. It works over almost any underlying network, because the nodes only need to reach each other. The cost is a small overhead per packet, a lower effective MTU and an extra layer to debug. Flannel with VXLAN is a simple example. Native routing A routed design gives pods addresses that the underlying network can route. Nodes advertise their pod ranges with BGP, as in Calico or Cilium in routing mode, or the cloud provider programs routes. There is no encapsulation, so performance is closer to the host network, and tools such as traceroute show the real path. It needs a network that cooperates, or cloud integration. eBPF dataplanes eBPF lets a plugin attach small verified programs to kernel hooks. Cilium uses it to implement forwarding, service load balancing and policy without long iptables chains, and can replace kube-proxy. Benefits are scale and visibility, with identity based policy and flow observability. The costs are kernel version requirements and a new way to debug. Features to compare Compare support for NetworkPolicy and any extensions, encryption between nodes, observability of flows, IPv6 and dual stack, multi-cluster connectivity, maximum cluster size and operational maturity. Check that the plugin supports your environment, for example a managed cloud with its own CNI. kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -E \"calico|cilium|flannel|weave\" ip -d link show | grep -E \"vxlan|geneve\" | head -3 ip route | head Choosing Start with requirements: network policy, scale, encryption, the base platform. A small lab can use a simple overlay. A production cluster usually wants policy support and good observability, which points to Calico or Cilium. Run the choice through a test in your environment before you commit, and plan how you will upgrade the plugin, since it runs on every node. CNI plugins: overlays, routing and eBPF Flow in order: 1, Overlay to Native routing; 2, Native routing to eBPF dataplane; 3, eBPF dataplane to Policy; 4, Policy to Observability. Step Security Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Overlay Step What it does: VXLAN or Geneve. If it fails or is weak: MTU too large. Native routing Step What it does: BGP or cloud routes. If it fails or is weak: Network refuses routes. eBPF dataplane Step What it does: Kernel programs. If it fails or is weak: Old kernel. Policy Security What it does: Enforced by the plugin. If it fails or is weak: Plugin without support. Observability Observability What it does: Flow visibility. If it fails or is weak: Not enabled. Incident: Large responses that hung Incident Small requests worked, but any response over a certain size stalled between pods on different nodes. $ kubectl exec web-1 -- ping -c1 -M do -s 1472 10.244.2.9 ping: local error: message too long, mtu=1450 $ ip link show vxlan.calico | grep mtu mtu 1450 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The overlay added headers, so the pod interface MTU was 1450, but the application tested paths with a larger size and relied on path MTU discovery that a firewall dropped. Small packets passed, and large ones stalled. Setting a consistent MTU across node and pod interfaces and allowing the relevant ICMP messages fixed it. Overlay overhead is a classic cause of \"works for small requests\" faults. Common mistakes Ignoring MTU with overlays Choosing a plugin without policy support when you need polic"},"k8s-network/services-clusterip-nodeport-and-kube-proxy":{"html":"\u003cp>Pods come and go and change IPs. A Service is the stable name and address in front of them. Understanding how a Service is turned into packet rules explains its behaviour, its limits and a good share of network faults.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Service types and what each exposes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How kube-proxy programs rules from Services and endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>iptables, IPVS and nftables modes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Session affinity, externalTrafficPolicy and source IPs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Headless Services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"types\">Types\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A ClusterIP Service is reachable only inside the cluster through a virtual IP. A NodePort adds a port, by default 30000 to 32767, on every node that forwards to the Service. A LoadBalancer asks the cloud provider for an external load balancer that targets the NodePorts. An ExternalName returns a DNS CNAME and does no proxying. A headless Service, with clusterIP None, has no virtual IP and returns the pod addresses in DNS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kube-proxy\">kube-proxy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>kube-proxy runs on every node, watches Services and EndpointSlices through the API server, and programs the node so that traffic to a ClusterIP and port is rewritten to a pod IP. It does not carry traffic itself in the common modes. In iptables mode it writes rules that select an endpoint at random, so large Services produce long rule lists. In IPVS mode it uses the kernel load balancer with hash tables, which scales better and offers more algorithms. Newer clusters can also use nftables mode.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"look-at-the-rules\">Look at the rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get svc web\nkubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web\nsudo iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES -n | head\nkubectl -n kube-system get cm kube-proxy -o jsonpath='{.data.config\\.conf}' | grep -E &quot;mode|ipvs&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get svc web\nkubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES -n | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\nkubectl -n kube-system get cm kube-proxy -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.data.config\\.conf}&#x27;\u003c/span> | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mode|ipvs&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"affinity-and-source-ips\">Affinity and source IPs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>By default each connection picks an endpoint independently. \u003ccode>sessionAffinity: ClientIP\u003c/code> keeps a client on the same pod for a period. For NodePort and LoadBalancer Services, \u003ccode>externalTrafficPolicy: Local\u003c/code> sends traffic only to pods on the receiving node and preserves the client source IP, but nodes without a local pod fail health checks. The default Cluster policy may hop to another node and replace the source IP.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"headless-services-and-stateful-sets\">Headless Services and stateful sets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A headless Service returns individual pod records, which is how StatefulSets give each pod a stable DNS name such as \u003ccode>db-0.db.prod.svc\u003c/code>. Clients that need to choose a member, such as a database driver, use these records.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"c&gt;rules;rules&gt;eps;rules&gt;pod\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Services: ClusterIP, NodePort and kube-proxy modes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"c\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rules\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kube-proxy rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"eps\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">EndpointSlices\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backend pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client pod to kube-proxy rules; 2, kube-proxy rules to EndpointSlices; 3, kube-proxy rules to Backend pod.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"c\">\u003cdt>Client pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Calls the ClusterIP.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong port.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rules\">\u003cdt>kube-proxy rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rewrite to a pod IP.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale rules.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"eps\">\u003cdt>EndpointSlices \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ready pod addresses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Empty list.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Backend pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Handles the request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not ready.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-service-with-no-endpoints\">Incident: A Service with no endpoints\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Calls to a Service hung, even though pods with the right name were running.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get svc cart\nkubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=cart\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get svc cart\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   TYPE        CLUSTER-IP     PORT(S)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cart   ClusterIP   10.96.44.18    80/TCP\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=cart\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME         ADDRESSTYPE   PORTS   ENDPOINTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cart-x7k2p   IPv4          80      &lt;unset&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The Service selector said app=cart but the pods carried app=cart-api, so no endpoints matched and connections to the ClusterIP had nowhere to go. Correcting the selector filled the EndpointSlice. When a Service misbehaves, check endpoints before anything else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Not checking endpoints first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Selector and label mismatch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using NodePort where an ingress is safer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming the source IP is preserved\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring readiness probes that keep pods out of endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"types","text":"Types"},{"id":"kube-proxy","text":"kube-proxy"},{"id":"look-at-the-rules","text":"Look at the rules"},{"id":"affinity-and-source-ips","text":"Affinity and source IPs"},{"id":"headless-services-and-stateful-sets","text":"Headless Services and stateful sets"},{"id":"incident-a-service-with-no-endpoints","text":"Incident: A Service with no endpoints"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Service","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/"},{"t":"Virtual IPs and service proxies","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/networking/virtual-ips/"}],"text":"Pods come and go and change IPs. A Service is the stable name and address in front of them. Understanding how a Service is turned into packet rules explains its behaviour, its limits and a good share of network faults. What you will learn Service types and what each exposes How kube-proxy programs rules from Services and endpoints iptables, IPVS and nftables modes Session affinity, externalTrafficPolicy and source IPs Headless Services Types A ClusterIP Service is reachable only inside the cluster through a virtual IP. A NodePort adds a port, by default 30000 to 32767, on every node that forwards to the Service. A LoadBalancer asks the cloud provider for an external load balancer that targets the NodePorts. An ExternalName returns a DNS CNAME and does no proxying. A headless Service, with clusterIP None, has no virtual IP and returns the pod addresses in DNS. kube-proxy kube-proxy runs on every node, watches Services and EndpointSlices through the API server, and programs the node so that traffic to a ClusterIP and port is rewritten to a pod IP. It does not carry traffic itself in the common modes. In iptables mode it writes rules that select an endpoint at random, so large Services produce long rule lists. In IPVS mode it uses the kernel load balancer with hash tables, which scales better and offers more algorithms. Newer clusters can also use nftables mode. Look at the rules kubectl get svc web kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web sudo iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES -n | head kubectl -n kube-system get cm kube-proxy -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.data.config\\.conf}&#x27; | grep -E \"mode|ipvs\" Affinity and source IPs By default each connection picks an endpoint independently. sessionAffinity: ClientIP keeps a client on the same pod for a period. For NodePort and LoadBalancer Services, externalTrafficPolicy: Local sends traffic only to pods on the receiving node and preserves the client source IP, but nodes without a local pod fail health checks. The default Cluster policy may hop to another node and replace the source IP. Headless Services and stateful sets A headless Service returns individual pod records, which is how StatefulSets give each pod a stable DNS name such as db-0.db.prod.svc . Clients that need to choose a member, such as a database driver, use these records. Services: ClusterIP, NodePort and kube-proxy modes Flow in order: 1, Client pod to kube-proxy rules; 2, kube-proxy rules to EndpointSlices; 3, kube-proxy rules to Backend pod. Outside party Step Data store Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Client pod Outside party What it does: Calls the ClusterIP. If it fails or is weak: Wrong port. kube-proxy rules Step What it does: Rewrite to a pod IP. If it fails or is weak: Stale rules. EndpointSlices Data store What it does: Ready pod addresses. If it fails or is weak: Empty list. Backend pod Service What it does: Handles the request. If it fails or is weak: Not ready. Incident: A Service with no endpoints Incident Calls to a Service hung, even though pods with the right name were running. $ kubectl get svc cart NAME TYPE CLUSTER-IP PORT(S) cart ClusterIP 10.96.44.18 80/TCP $ kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=cart NAME ADDRESSTYPE PORTS ENDPOINTS cart-x7k2p IPv4 80 \u003cunset> (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The Service selector said app=cart but the pods carried app=cart-api, so no endpoints matched and connections to the ClusterIP had nowhere to go. Correcting the selector filled the EndpointSlice. When a Service misbehaves, check endpoints before anything else. Common mistakes Not checking endpoints first Selector and label mismatch Using NodePort where an ingress is safer Assuming the source IP is preserved Ignoring readiness probes that keep pods out of endpoints"},"k8s-network/endpointslices-and-how-traffic-finds-pods":{"html":"\u003cp>Between a Service and a pod there is a list. That list, the EndpointSlice, is what really decides where traffic goes, and many apparent network problems are in fact wrong lists.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why EndpointSlices replaced Endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How the controller builds slices from selectors and readiness\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Terminating endpoints and graceful shutdown\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Topology aware routing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Services without selectors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"from-endpoints-to-slices\">From Endpoints to slices\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The old Endpoints object held every address of a Service in one resource, so a large Service made every update huge and sent it to every node. EndpointSlices split addresses into slices, by default up to 100 each, so an update changes one small object. kube-proxy and other consumers watch slices.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-slices-are-built\">How slices are built\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The EndpointSlice controller watches Services and pods. For each Service with a selector it lists matching pods and records their IP, port, node, zone and conditions. A pod is ready only if its containers pass readiness. Only ready endpoints receive new connections in normal operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conditions\">Conditions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each endpoint has ready, serving and terminating conditions. During a rolling update a pod that is shutting down becomes terminating, and stays serving until it exits if its readiness still passes, which helps drain connections. Pair this with a preStop delay and a termination grace period so that load balancers and kube-proxy have time to stop sending new traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"zone-aware-routing\">Zone aware routing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With topology aware routing, enabled by annotation or the traffic distribution field, a Service prefers endpoints in the same zone, which reduces latency and cross zone cost. It falls back when a zone has too few endpoints, so make sure replicas are spread across zones.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get endpointslice -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web -o yaml | head -40\nkubectl get pods -l app=web -o wide\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get endpointslice -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web -o yaml | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -40\nkubectl get pods -l app=web -o wide\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"selectorless-services\">Selectorless Services\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Service without a selector gets no automatic slices. You create an EndpointSlice by hand to point at something outside the cluster, such as a database VM. It is also how some controllers publish backends, and a missing slice is the explanation for such Services failing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"svc&gt;ctl;ctl&gt;slice;slice&gt;kp;kp&gt;pod\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">EndpointSlices and how traffic finds pods\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service selector\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctl\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Slice controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"slice\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">EndpointSlice\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kp\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kube-proxy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ready pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Service selector to Slice controller; 2, Slice controller to EndpointSlice; 3, EndpointSlice to kube-proxy; 4, kube-proxy to Ready pods.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service selector \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Matches labels.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Mismatch.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctl\">\u003cdt>Slice controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Builds the list.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lagging.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"slice\">\u003cdt>EndpointSlice \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ready addresses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Empty.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kp\">\u003cdt>kube-proxy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Programs nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Ready pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receive traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not ready.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-errors-during-every-rolling-update\">Incident: Errors during every rolling update\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Clients saw connection resets for a few seconds on each deploy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get deploy web -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.terminationGracePeriodSeconds}'\nkubectl get deploy web -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].lifecycle}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy web -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.terminationGracePeriodSeconds}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy web -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].lifecycle}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(no output)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The container stopped accepting connections the moment it received SIGTERM, while kube-proxy and the load balancer still sent a few more. Adding a short preStop sleep gave the endpoint removal time to spread before shutdown began, and the resets stopped. Graceful shutdown is a network feature as well as an application one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ignoring readiness probes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stopping the listener immediately on SIGTERM\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spreading replicas in one zone with zone routing on\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating Endpoints by hand instead of slices\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming propagation of endpoint changes is instant\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"from-endpoints-to-slices","text":"From Endpoints to slices"},{"id":"how-slices-are-built","text":"How slices are built"},{"id":"conditions","text":"Conditions"},{"id":"zone-aware-routing","text":"Zone aware routing"},{"id":"selectorless-services","text":"Selectorless Services"},{"id":"incident-errors-during-every-rolling-update","text":"Incident: Errors during every rolling update"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"EndpointSlices","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/endpoint-slices/"},{"t":"Pod lifecycle","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-lifecycle/"}],"text":"Between a Service and a pod there is a list. That list, the EndpointSlice, is what really decides where traffic goes, and many apparent network problems are in fact wrong lists. What you will learn Why EndpointSlices replaced Endpoints How the controller builds slices from selectors and readiness Terminating endpoints and graceful shutdown Topology aware routing Services without selectors From Endpoints to slices The old Endpoints object held every address of a Service in one resource, so a large Service made every update huge and sent it to every node. EndpointSlices split addresses into slices, by default up to 100 each, so an update changes one small object. kube-proxy and other consumers watch slices. How slices are built The EndpointSlice controller watches Services and pods. For each Service with a selector it lists matching pods and records their IP, port, node, zone and conditions. A pod is ready only if its containers pass readiness. Only ready endpoints receive new connections in normal operation. Conditions Each endpoint has ready, serving and terminating conditions. During a rolling update a pod that is shutting down becomes terminating, and stays serving until it exits if its readiness still passes, which helps drain connections. Pair this with a preStop delay and a termination grace period so that load balancers and kube-proxy have time to stop sending new traffic. Zone aware routing With topology aware routing, enabled by annotation or the traffic distribution field, a Service prefers endpoints in the same zone, which reduces latency and cross zone cost. It falls back when a zone has too few endpoints, so make sure replicas are spread across zones. kubectl get endpointslice -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web -o yaml | head -40 kubectl get pods -l app=web -o wide Selectorless Services A Service without a selector gets no automatic slices. You create an EndpointSlice by hand to point at something outside the cluster, such as a database VM. It is also how some controllers publish backends, and a missing slice is the explanation for such Services failing. EndpointSlices and how traffic finds pods Flow in order: 1, Service selector to Slice controller; 2, Slice controller to EndpointSlice; 3, EndpointSlice to kube-proxy; 4, kube-proxy to Ready pods. Data store Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Service selector Data store What it does: Matches labels. If it fails or is weak: Mismatch. Slice controller Step What it does: Builds the list. If it fails or is weak: Lagging. EndpointSlice Data store What it does: Ready addresses. If it fails or is weak: Empty. kube-proxy Service What it does: Programs nodes. If it fails or is weak: Stale. Ready pods Service What it does: Receive traffic. If it fails or is weak: Not ready. Incident: Errors during every rolling update Incident Clients saw connection resets for a few seconds on each deploy. $ kubectl get deploy web -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.template.spec.terminationGracePeriodSeconds}&#x27; 30 $ kubectl get deploy web -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].lifecycle}&#x27; (no output) (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The container stopped accepting connections the moment it received SIGTERM, while kube-proxy and the load balancer still sent a few more. Adding a short preStop sleep gave the endpoint removal time to spread before shutdown began, and the resets stopped. Graceful shutdown is a network feature as well as an application one. Common mistakes Ignoring readiness probes Stopping the listener immediately on SIGTERM Spreading replicas in one zone with zone routing on Creating Endpoints by hand instead of slices Assuming propagation of endpoint changes is instant"},"k8s-network/coredns-and-service-discovery":{"html":"\u003cp>Almost every request in a cluster starts with a DNS lookup. DNS faults look like network faults, and slow DNS looks like slow applications. A clear picture of how names resolve removes a lot of guesswork.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Service and pod DNS names\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What is in a pod resolv.conf and why ndots matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The CoreDNS Corefile and plugins\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scaling and caching\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging resolution\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"names\">Names\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Service named web in namespace shop has the name \u003ccode>web.shop.svc.cluster.local\u003c/code>. Pods in the same namespace can use just \u003ccode>web\u003c/code>, and from other namespaces \u003ccode>web.shop\u003c/code>. Headless Services also publish per pod names. SRV records carry ports. Pods have IP based names that are rarely used.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resolv-conf-and-ndots\">resolv.conf and ndots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The kubelet writes each pod resolv.conf with the cluster DNS Service IP as nameserver, a search list such as \u003ccode>shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>options ndots:5\u003c/code>. A name with fewer than five dots is tried with each search suffix first, so a lookup for \u003ccode>api.example.com\u003c/code> makes several failing queries before the real one. Using a fully qualified name with a trailing dot, or lowering ndots in the pod spec, cuts that traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-corefile\">The Corefile\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>CoreDNS runs as a Deployment in kube-system, behind the kube-dns Service. Its configuration is the Corefile in a ConfigMap, built from plugins. The kubernetes plugin answers cluster names from the API. The forward plugin sends other names to upstream resolvers. The cache plugin caches answers, and loop detects forwarding loops. You can add stub domains and custom zones.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl -n kube-system get cm coredns -o jsonpath='{.data.Corefile}'\nkubectl -n kube-system get deploy coredns\nkubectl -n kube-system logs -l k8s-app=kube-dns --tail=20\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl -n kube-system get cm coredns -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.data.Corefile}&#x27;\u003c/span>\nkubectl -n kube-system get deploy coredns\nkubectl -n kube-system logs -l k8s-app=kube-dns --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>=20\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scale-and-cache\">Scale and cache\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Large clusters send heavy DNS traffic. Scale CoreDNS with replicas or autoscaling relative to cluster size, and consider NodeLocal DNSCache, a per node cache that reduces latency and conntrack pressure. Watch the request rate, cache hit ratio and error counts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"debugging\">Debugging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Test from a throwaway pod rather than from the node, because the node resolver differs. Check the nameserver in resolv.conf, query a cluster name and an external name, and read CoreDNS logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never --rm -it -- nslookup web.shop.svc.cluster.local\nkubectl exec web-1 -- cat /etc/resolv.conf\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it -- nslookup web.shop.svc.cluster.local\nkubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-1 -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /etc/resolv.conf\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"pod&gt;dns;dns&gt;api;dns&gt;up\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">CoreDNS and service discovery\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod resolver\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dns\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CoreDNS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"up\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Upstream DNS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pod resolver to CoreDNS; 2, CoreDNS to API server; 3, CoreDNS to Upstream DNS.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod resolver \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Uses search and ndots.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Many wasted queries.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dns\">\u003cdt>CoreDNS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Answers cluster names.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overloaded.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Source of Services.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"up\">\u003cdt>Upstream DNS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> External names.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unreachable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-dns-slowness-from-search-domains\">Incident: DNS slowness from search domains\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An application that called an external API had five second delays on its first call each minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec web-1 -- cat /etc/resolv.conf\nkubectl -n kube-system logs -l k8s-app=kube-dns --tail=2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-1 -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /etc/resolv.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">search shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local corp.example\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nameserver 10.96.0.10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">options ndots:5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl -n kube-system logs -l k8s-app=kube-dns --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>=2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[ERROR] plugin/errors: 2 api.partner.com.corp.example. A: read udp 10.244.1.4:41872-&gt;10.0.0.2:53: i/o timeout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A lookup for api.partner.com with ndots 5 tried each search suffix first, and one of them, the corporate suffix, went to an upstream resolver that dropped queries. Each timeout added seconds. Using the fully qualified name with a trailing dot, and lowering ndots for that workload, removed the delay. Search domains are a hidden multiplier on DNS load.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ignoring ndots and search domains\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing DNS from the node instead of a pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving CoreDNS at two replicas in a large cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing the Corefile without checking the loop plugin\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blaming the network for resolution errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"names","text":"Names"},{"id":"resolv-conf-and-ndots","text":"resolv.conf and ndots"},{"id":"the-corefile","text":"The Corefile"},{"id":"scale-and-cache","text":"Scale and cache"},{"id":"debugging","text":"Debugging"},{"id":"incident-dns-slowness-from-search-domains","text":"Incident: DNS slowness from search domains"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"DNS for services and pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/"},{"t":"Debugging DNS resolution","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/dns-debugging-resolution/"}],"text":"Almost every request in a cluster starts with a DNS lookup. DNS faults look like network faults, and slow DNS looks like slow applications. A clear picture of how names resolve removes a lot of guesswork. What you will learn Service and pod DNS names What is in a pod resolv.conf and why ndots matters The CoreDNS Corefile and plugins Scaling and caching Debugging resolution Names A Service named web in namespace shop has the name web.shop.svc.cluster.local . Pods in the same namespace can use just web , and from other namespaces web.shop . Headless Services also publish per pod names. SRV records carry ports. Pods have IP based names that are rarely used. resolv.conf and ndots The kubelet writes each pod resolv.conf with the cluster DNS Service IP as nameserver, a search list such as shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local , and options ndots:5 . A name with fewer than five dots is tried with each search suffix first, so a lookup for api.example.com makes several failing queries before the real one. Using a fully qualified name with a trailing dot, or lowering ndots in the pod spec, cuts that traffic. The Corefile CoreDNS runs as a Deployment in kube-system, behind the kube-dns Service. Its configuration is the Corefile in a ConfigMap, built from plugins. The kubernetes plugin answers cluster names from the API. The forward plugin sends other names to upstream resolvers. The cache plugin caches answers, and loop detects forwarding loops. You can add stub domains and custom zones. kubectl -n kube-system get cm coredns -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.data.Corefile}&#x27; kubectl -n kube-system get deploy coredns kubectl -n kube-system logs -l k8s-app=kube-dns -- tail =20 Scale and cache Large clusters send heavy DNS traffic. Scale CoreDNS with replicas or autoscaling relative to cluster size, and consider NodeLocal DNSCache, a per node cache that reduces latency and conntrack pressure. Watch the request rate, cache hit ratio and error counts. Debugging Test from a throwaway pod rather than from the node, because the node resolver differs. Check the nameserver in resolv.conf, query a cluster name and an external name, and read CoreDNS logs. kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- rm -it -- nslookup web.shop.svc.cluster.local kubectl exec web-1 -- cat /etc/resolv.conf CoreDNS and service discovery Flow in order: 1, Pod resolver to CoreDNS; 2, CoreDNS to API server; 3, CoreDNS to Upstream DNS. Outside party Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pod resolver Outside party What it does: Uses search and ndots. If it fails or is weak: Many wasted queries. CoreDNS Service What it does: Answers cluster names. If it fails or is weak: Overloaded. API server Service What it does: Source of Services. If it fails or is weak: Slow. Upstream DNS Outside party What it does: External names. If it fails or is weak: Unreachable. Incident: DNS slowness from search domains Incident An application that called an external API had five second delays on its first call each minute. $ kubectl exec web-1 -- cat /etc/resolv.conf search shop.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local corp.example nameserver 10.96.0.10 options ndots:5 $ kubectl -n kube-system logs -l k8s-app=kube-dns -- tail =2 [ERROR] plugin/errors: 2 api.partner.com.corp.example. A: read udp 10.244.1.4:41872->10.0.0.2:53: i/o timeout (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A lookup for api.partner.com with ndots 5 tried each search suffix first, and one of them, the corporate suffix, went to an upstream resolver that dropped queries. Each timeout added seconds. Using the fully qualified name with a trailing dot, and lowering ndots for that workload, removed the delay. Search domains are a hidden multiplier on DNS load. Common mistakes Ignoring ndots and search domains Testing DNS from the node instead of a pod Leaving CoreDNS at two replicas in a large cluster Edi"},"k8s-network/network-policies-in-depth":{"html":"\u003cp>By default every pod can talk to every other pod. Network policies are the mechanism to limit that, but only if the CNI enforces them, and their selector rules are easy to get subtly wrong.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The default allow behaviour and what changes when a pod is selected\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ingress and egress rules and how they combine\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>podSelector, namespaceSelector and ipBlock\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The classic mistake with two selectors in one rule\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing and patterns\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"selection-and-the-model\">Selection and the model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A NetworkPolicy selects pods with a podSelector. Pods that no policy selects stay open. As soon as a pod is selected by a policy that lists a policy type, the pod becomes isolated for that direction, and only traffic allowed by some policy is permitted. Policies are additive, so there is no deny rule: the union of all allow rules applies. An empty podSelector selects all pods in the namespace, which is how you write default deny.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"default-deny-and-allow\">Default deny and allow\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write a default deny for ingress, egress or both, then add allow policies for each flow. Remember DNS: with default deny egress, pods can no longer resolve names, so allow UDP and TCP port 53 to the DNS pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [Ingress, Egress]\n---\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: allow-dns\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [Egress]\n  egress:\n    - to:\n        - namespaceSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system\n      ports:\n        - {protocol: UDP, port: 53}\n        - {protocol: TCP, port: 53}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default-deny\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Egress\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">allow-dns\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Egress\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">egress:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">to:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespaceSelector:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kubernetes.io/metadata.name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kube-system\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">protocol:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">UDP\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">53\u003c/span>}\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">protocol:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">TCP\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">53\u003c/span>}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"peers\">Peers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A rule can name peers with podSelector for pods in the policy namespace, namespaceSelector for pods in matching namespaces, both together for specific pods in specific namespaces, or ipBlock for CIDR ranges with optional exceptions. Namespaces carry the label \u003ccode>kubernetes.io/metadata.name\u003c/code>, which makes selecting one by name easy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-item-or-two\">One item or two\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In a \u003ccode>from\u003c/code> list, two selectors in the same item must both match, an AND. Two separate list items are alternatives, an OR. A dash in the wrong place changes the meaning completely, often allowing far more than intended. Read the indentation of every rule.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\nkubectl describe networkpolicy allow-web -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\nkubectl describe networkpolicy allow-web -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"testing-and-patterns\">Testing and patterns\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Test from a throwaway pod with the labels of the client you are testing. Check both an allowed and a denied flow. Common patterns: default deny per namespace, allow from the ingress controller namespace, allow to the database only from the app tier, and egress allow lists for external APIs. Remember that policies are only enforced if the CNI supports them, and that some policies act on node or host traffic differently.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"pol&gt;ing;pol&gt;eg;ing&gt;cni;eg&gt;cni;cni&gt;test\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Network policies in depth\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ing\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ingress rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"eg\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Egress rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cni\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CNI enforcement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Test pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Policy to Ingress rules; 2, Policy to Egress rules; 3, Ingress rules to CNI enforcement; 4, Egress rules to CNI enforcement; 5, CNI enforcement to Test pods.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Selects pods.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Empty selector.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ing\">\u003cdt>Ingress rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Who may connect in.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too wide.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"eg\">\u003cdt>Egress rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Where pods may go.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> DNS blocked.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cni\">\u003cdt>CNI enforcement \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Applies the rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not supported.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>Test pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Prove allow and deny.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not tested.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-dash-too-many\">Incident: One dash too many\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A policy intended to allow the frontend only ended up allowing every pod in the frontend namespace and every pod labelled web anywhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get networkpolicy allow-web -n db -o yaml | grep -A8 &quot;from:&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get networkpolicy allow-web -n db -o yaml | grep -A8 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;from:&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  - from:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    - namespaceSelector:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        matchLabels:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">          team: frontend\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    - podSelector:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        matchLabels:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">          app: web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Two list items mean OR. The author wanted pods labelled app web in namespaces labelled team frontend, which needs both selectors in one item. The loose policy let any pod in the frontend namespace and any web pod in the database namespace connect. Correcting the indentation to a single item restored the intended AND. Review every from and to list for dashes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Forgetting DNS in a default deny egress policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using two list items where one item with two selectors is meant\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting deny rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a CNI that does not enforce policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not testing both allowed and denied flows\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"selection-and-the-model","text":"Selection and the model"},{"id":"default-deny-and-allow","text":"Default deny and allow"},{"id":"peers","text":"Peers"},{"id":"one-item-or-two","text":"One item or two"},{"id":"testing-and-patterns","text":"Testing and patterns"},{"id":"incident-one-dash-too-many","text":"Incident: One dash too many"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Network policies","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/"},{"t":"Declare network policy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/declare-network-policy/"}],"text":"By default every pod can talk to every other pod. Network policies are the mechanism to limit that, but only if the CNI enforces them, and their selector rules are easy to get subtly wrong. What you will learn The default allow behaviour and what changes when a pod is selected Ingress and egress rules and how they combine podSelector, namespaceSelector and ipBlock The classic mistake with two selectors in one rule Testing and patterns Selection and the model A NetworkPolicy selects pods with a podSelector. Pods that no policy selects stay open. As soon as a pod is selected by a policy that lists a policy type, the pod becomes isolated for that direction, and only traffic allowed by some policy is permitted. Policies are additive, so there is no deny rule: the union of all allow rules applies. An empty podSelector selects all pods in the namespace, which is how you write default deny. Default deny and allow Write a default deny for ingress, egress or both, then add allow policies for each flow. Remember DNS: with default deny egress, pods can no longer resolve names, so allow UDP and TCP port 53 to the DNS pods. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny namespace: shop spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: [ Ingress , Egress ] --- apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: allow-dns namespace: shop spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: [ Egress ] egress: - to: - namespaceSelector: matchLabels: kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system ports: - { protocol: UDP , port: 53 } - { protocol: TCP , port: 53 } Peers A rule can name peers with podSelector for pods in the policy namespace, namespaceSelector for pods in matching namespaces, both together for specific pods in specific namespaces, or ipBlock for CIDR ranges with optional exceptions. Namespaces carry the label kubernetes.io/metadata.name , which makes selecting one by name easy. One item or two In a from list, two selectors in the same item must both match, an AND. Two separate list items are alternatives, an OR. A dash in the wrong place changes the meaning completely, often allowing far more than intended. Read the indentation of every rule. kubectl get networkpolicy -n shop kubectl describe networkpolicy allow-web -n shop Testing and patterns Test from a throwaway pod with the labels of the client you are testing. Check both an allowed and a denied flow. Common patterns: default deny per namespace, allow from the ingress controller namespace, allow to the database only from the app tier, and egress allow lists for external APIs. Remember that policies are only enforced if the CNI supports them, and that some policies act on node or host traffic differently. Network policies in depth Flow in order: 1, Policy to Ingress rules; 2, Policy to Egress rules; 3, Ingress rules to CNI enforcement; 4, Egress rules to CNI enforcement; 5, CNI enforcement to Test pods. Security Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Policy Security What it does: Selects pods. If it fails or is weak: Empty selector. Ingress rules Security What it does: Who may connect in. If it fails or is weak: Too wide. Egress rules Security What it does: Where pods may go. If it fails or is weak: DNS blocked. CNI enforcement Step What it does: Applies the rules. If it fails or is weak: Not supported. Test pods Observability What it does: Prove allow and deny. If it fails or is weak: Not tested. Incident: One dash too many Incident A policy intended to allow the frontend only ended up allowing every pod in the frontend namespace and every pod labelled web anywhere. $ kubectl get networkpolicy allow-web -n db -o yaml | grep -A8 \"from:\" - from: - namespaceSelector: matchLabels: team: frontend - podSelector: matchLabels: app: web (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Two list items mean OR. The author wanted pods labelled app web in namespaces labelled "},"k8s-network/ingress-and-the-gateway-api":{"html":"\u003cp>A Service gets traffic to pods. Ingress and the Gateway API take HTTP and HTTPS from outside the cluster and route it to the right Service by host and path. They are objects in the API, but they do nothing without a controller that implements them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ingress objects, classes and controllers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>TLS termination and certificates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Gateway, GatewayClass and routes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Role separation and cross namespace routing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing between them and migration\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ingress\">Ingress\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An Ingress declares host and path rules that map to Services, plus TLS settings. An ingress controller, such as ingress-nginx, Traefik or a cloud controller, watches Ingress objects and configures a proxy or load balancer. Which controller handles an object is chosen with \u003ccode>ingressClassName\u003c/code>. Without a controller, the Ingress is just data. Controller specific behaviour is added through annotations, which makes manifests less portable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tls\">TLS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ingress can terminate TLS using a certificate stored in a Secret of type kubernetes.io/tls, referenced in the \u003ccode>tls\u003c/code> section. Tools such as cert-manager request and renew certificates automatically. The proxy then forwards plain or re-encrypted traffic to the backends.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: web\nspec:\n  ingressClassName: nginx\n  tls:\n    - hosts: [shop.example.com]\n      secretName: shop-tls\n  rules:\n    - host: shop.example.com\n      http:\n        paths:\n          - path: /\n            pathType: Prefix\n            backend:\n              service:\n                name: web\n                port:\n                  number: 80\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingressClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tls:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>]\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secretName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-tls\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pathType:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Prefix\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backend:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service:\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span>\n                  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">number:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gateway-api\">Gateway API\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The Gateway API splits responsibilities across objects. A GatewayClass names a controller implementation, managed by the platform provider. A Gateway describes listeners, such as ports, protocols and TLS, and is owned by the cluster operator. Routes such as HTTPRoute attach to a Gateway and describe how requests reach Services, owned by application teams. The model supports header matching, traffic splitting, redirects and other features in the spec instead of annotations.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sharing-safely\">Sharing safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Gateways can restrict which namespaces may attach routes, and cross namespace references need a ReferenceGrant. This lets a platform team run a shared Gateway while teams manage only their own routes, which Ingress could not express cleanly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ingress is mature, widely supported and enough for simple host and path routing. The Gateway API is the direction of the project, with richer features and a better ownership model, and is the choice for new designs when your controller supports it. Both need a controller you operate and monitor. Check events and the status of the objects when traffic does not arrive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get ingressclass\nkubectl describe ingress web\nkubectl get gateway,httproute -A\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get ingressclass\nkubectl describe ingress web\nkubectl get gateway,httproute -A\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"cl&gt;lb;lb&gt;ctl;ctl&gt;route;ctl&gt;svc\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Ingress and the Gateway API\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cl\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lb\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load balancer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctl\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Controller and proxy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"route\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:25.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ingress or HTTPRoute\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service and pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client to Load balancer; 2, Load balancer to Controller and proxy; 3, Controller and proxy to Ingress or HTTPRoute; 4, Controller and proxy to Service and pods.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cl\">\u003cdt>Client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> HTTPS request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong DNS.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lb\">\u003cdt>Load balancer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Entry point.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Health check fails.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctl\">\u003cdt>Controller and proxy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Implements the rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing class.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"route\">\u003cdt>Ingress or HTTPRoute \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Host and path rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong backend.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service and pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Backends.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No endpoints.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-an-ingress-that-nothing-served\">Incident: An Ingress that nothing served\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new Ingress showed no address for days.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get ingress web\nkubectl get ingressclass\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get ingress web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   CLASS    HOSTS              ADDRESS   PORTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web    &lt;none&gt;   shop.example.com             80\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get ingressclass\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME    CONTROLLER                     PARAMETERS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nginx   k8s.io/ingress-nginx           &lt;none&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The Ingress had no class and there was no default class, so no controller claimed it. Setting ingressClassName to nginx made the controller pick it up and the address appeared. An Ingress with an empty address often just needs the right class, so check the class before debugging the proxy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Creating an Ingress without a controller or class\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on controller specific annotations for portability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that TLS Secrets must be in the same namespace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exposing a Service as NodePort instead of through a gateway\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not checking the status of Gateway objects\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"ingress","text":"Ingress"},{"id":"tls","text":"TLS"},{"id":"gateway-api","text":"Gateway API"},{"id":"sharing-safely","text":"Sharing safely"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"incident-an-ingress-that-nothing-served","text":"Incident: An Ingress that nothing served"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Ingress","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ingress/"},{"t":"Gateway API","u":"https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/"}],"text":"A Service gets traffic to pods. Ingress and the Gateway API take HTTP and HTTPS from outside the cluster and route it to the right Service by host and path. They are objects in the API, but they do nothing without a controller that implements them. What you will learn Ingress objects, classes and controllers TLS termination and certificates Gateway, GatewayClass and routes Role separation and cross namespace routing Choosing between them and migration Ingress An Ingress declares host and path rules that map to Services, plus TLS settings. An ingress controller, such as ingress-nginx, Traefik or a cloud controller, watches Ingress objects and configures a proxy or load balancer. Which controller handles an object is chosen with ingressClassName . Without a controller, the Ingress is just data. Controller specific behaviour is added through annotations, which makes manifests less portable. TLS Ingress can terminate TLS using a certificate stored in a Secret of type kubernetes.io/tls, referenced in the tls section. Tools such as cert-manager request and renew certificates automatically. The proxy then forwards plain or re-encrypted traffic to the backends. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: web spec: ingressClassName: nginx tls: - hosts: [ shop.example.com ] secretName: shop-tls rules: - host: shop.example.com http: paths: - path: / pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: web port: number: 80 Gateway API The Gateway API splits responsibilities across objects. A GatewayClass names a controller implementation, managed by the platform provider. A Gateway describes listeners, such as ports, protocols and TLS, and is owned by the cluster operator. Routes such as HTTPRoute attach to a Gateway and describe how requests reach Services, owned by application teams. The model supports header matching, traffic splitting, redirects and other features in the spec instead of annotations. Sharing safely Gateways can restrict which namespaces may attach routes, and cross namespace references need a ReferenceGrant. This lets a platform team run a shared Gateway while teams manage only their own routes, which Ingress could not express cleanly. Choosing Ingress is mature, widely supported and enough for simple host and path routing. The Gateway API is the direction of the project, with richer features and a better ownership model, and is the choice for new designs when your controller supports it. Both need a controller you operate and monitor. Check events and the status of the objects when traffic does not arrive. kubectl get ingressclass kubectl describe ingress web kubectl get gateway,httproute -A Ingress and the Gateway API Flow in order: 1, Client to Load balancer; 2, Load balancer to Controller and proxy; 3, Controller and proxy to Ingress or HTTPRoute; 4, Controller and proxy to Service and pods. Outside party Service Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Client Outside party What it does: HTTPS request. If it fails or is weak: Wrong DNS. Load balancer Service What it does: Entry point. If it fails or is weak: Health check fails. Controller and proxy Step What it does: Implements the rules. If it fails or is weak: Missing class. Ingress or HTTPRoute Data store What it does: Host and path rules. If it fails or is weak: Wrong backend. Service and pods Service What it does: Backends. If it fails or is weak: No endpoints. Incident: An Ingress that nothing served Incident A new Ingress showed no address for days. $ kubectl get ingress web NAME CLASS HOSTS ADDRESS PORTS web \u003cnone> shop.example.com 80 $ kubectl get ingressclass NAME CONTROLLER PARAMETERS nginx k8s.io/ingress-nginx \u003cnone> (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The Ingress had no class and there was no default class, so no controller claimed it. Setting ingressClassName to nginx made the controller pick it up and the address appeared. An Ingress wi"},"k8s-network/debugging-kubernetes-networking":{"html":"\u003cp>Network faults are easy to chase in circles. A fixed method, working from the symptom to the layer, saves time and works in an exam as well as in production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Name the traffic type and the exact failing hop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A layered checklist: DNS, Service, endpoints, pod, policy, node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tools: ephemeral debug containers, nicolaka netshoot, tcpdump, conntrack\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading conntrack and kube-proxy rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A worked sequence\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-the-question\">Start with the question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Say precisely what fails: from which pod to which name and port, and what error you see. Connection refused means something answered with a reset, usually no listener. A timeout means packets are dropped or lost, so look at policy, routing or the endpoint being gone. Name resolution errors mean DNS. A TLS error is above the network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-layered-order\">The layered order\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Test in this order and stop at the first failure. One, can the client resolve the name. Two, does the Service exist with the right port and selector. Three, does it have ready endpoints. Four, can you reach a pod IP directly, which bypasses the Service. Five, does a policy block the flow. Six, is the node level path healthy: routes, kube-proxy rules, conntrack and MTU.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec client -- nslookup web.shop\nkubectl get svc,endpointslices -n shop\nkubectl exec client -- curl -s -m 3 http://10.244.2.9:8080/healthz\nkubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> client -- nslookup web.shop\nkubectl get svc,endpointslices -n shop\nkubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> client -- curl -s -m 3 http://10.244.2.9:8080/healthz\nkubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tools-in-the-cluster\">Tools in the cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A minimal image often has no tools. Use an ephemeral debug container to attach to a running pod, or run a toolbox pod such as netshoot with curl, dig, tcpdump and ss. Debug on the same node as the failing pod when node level behaviour matters.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl debug -it web-1 --image=nicolaka/netshoot --target=app\nkubectl run tools --image=nicolaka/netshoot --restart=Never -- sleep 3600\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl debug -it web-1 --image=nicolaka/netshoot --target=app\nkubectl run tools --image=nicolaka/netshoot --restart=Never -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sleep\u003c/span> 3600\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"node-level-checks\">Node level checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>On the node, \u003ccode>ip route\u003c/code> shows pod routes, \u003ccode>ss -tnp\u003c/code> shows listeners, \u003ccode>iptables-save | grep KUBE-SVC\u003c/code> shows Service chains, and \u003ccode>conntrack -S\u003c/code> shows table statistics. A full conntrack table or insert failures cause random connection errors under load. UDP DNS traffic is a common victim.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-worked-sequence\">A worked sequence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pod times out calling \u003ccode>cart\u003c/code>. Name resolves, the Service has a ClusterIP, endpoints are empty, the selector says app=cart and the pods say app=cart-api. The fix is the selector. If endpoints had been present, the next test would have been a direct call to a pod IP, then policy, then the node. The method is the same each time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"sym&gt;dns;dns&gt;svc;svc&gt;pol;pol&gt;node\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Debugging Kubernetes networking\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sym\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Symptom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dns\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">DNS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service and endpoints\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy and routes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Node and conntrack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Symptom to DNS; 2, DNS to Service and endpoints; 3, Service and endpoints to Policy and routes; 4, Policy and routes to Node and conntrack.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sym\">\u003cdt>Symptom \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Refused or timed out?\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dns\">\u003cdt>DNS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Name resolves.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> ndots, CoreDNS.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service and endpoints \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ports and backends.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Selector.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policy and routes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allowed and routed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Silent drops.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>Node and conntrack \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rules and tables.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Table full.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-random-resets-under-load\">Incident: Random resets under load\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A busy service had random connection failures that no policy or Service change explained.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo conntrack -S | head -3\nsudo sysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> conntrack -S | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cpu=0 found=912 invalid=3 insert=4410 insert_failed=1873 drop=1873\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max = 131072\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count = 131060\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The connection tracking table was full, so new connections were dropped at random. Raising nf_conntrack_max, reducing short lived connections with keepalives and moving DNS to a node local cache fixed the faults. When symptoms are random and load dependent, check node level tables before changing application settings.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping the Service and endpoint checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing only from a laptop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing several things at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming a timeout is the same as a refusal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring node level limits such as conntrack\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-the-question","text":"Start with the question"},{"id":"the-layered-order","text":"The layered order"},{"id":"tools-in-the-cluster","text":"Tools in the cluster"},{"id":"node-level-checks","text":"Node level checks"},{"id":"a-worked-sequence","text":"A worked sequence"},{"id":"incident-random-resets-under-load","text":"Incident: Random resets under load"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Debug services","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-service/"},{"t":"Debugging DNS resolution","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/dns-debugging-resolution/"}],"text":"Network faults are easy to chase in circles. A fixed method, working from the symptom to the layer, saves time and works in an exam as well as in production. What you will learn Name the traffic type and the exact failing hop A layered checklist: DNS, Service, endpoints, pod, policy, node Tools: ephemeral debug containers, nicolaka netshoot, tcpdump, conntrack Reading conntrack and kube-proxy rules A worked sequence Start with the question Say precisely what fails: from which pod to which name and port, and what error you see. Connection refused means something answered with a reset, usually no listener. A timeout means packets are dropped or lost, so look at policy, routing or the endpoint being gone. Name resolution errors mean DNS. A TLS error is above the network. The layered order Test in this order and stop at the first failure. One, can the client resolve the name. Two, does the Service exist with the right port and selector. Three, does it have ready endpoints. Four, can you reach a pod IP directly, which bypasses the Service. Five, does a policy block the flow. Six, is the node level path healthy: routes, kube-proxy rules, conntrack and MTU. kubectl exec client -- nslookup web.shop kubectl get svc,endpointslices -n shop kubectl exec client -- curl -s -m 3 http://10.244.2.9:8080/healthz kubectl get networkpolicy -n shop Tools in the cluster A minimal image often has no tools. Use an ephemeral debug container to attach to a running pod, or run a toolbox pod such as netshoot with curl, dig, tcpdump and ss. Debug on the same node as the failing pod when node level behaviour matters. kubectl debug -it web-1 --image=nicolaka/netshoot --target=app kubectl run tools --image=nicolaka/netshoot --restart=Never -- sleep 3600 Node level checks On the node, ip route shows pod routes, ss -tnp shows listeners, iptables-save | grep KUBE-SVC shows Service chains, and conntrack -S shows table statistics. A full conntrack table or insert failures cause random connection errors under load. UDP DNS traffic is a common victim. A worked sequence A pod times out calling cart . Name resolves, the Service has a ClusterIP, endpoints are empty, the selector says app=cart and the pods say app=cart-api. The fix is the selector. If endpoints had been present, the next test would have been a direct call to a pod IP, then policy, then the node. The method is the same each time. Debugging Kubernetes networking Flow in order: 1, Symptom to DNS; 2, DNS to Service and endpoints; 3, Service and endpoints to Policy and routes; 4, Policy and routes to Node and conntrack. Observability Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Symptom Observability What it does: Refused or timed out? If it fails or is weak: Vague. DNS Step What it does: Name resolves. If it fails or is weak: ndots, CoreDNS. Service and endpoints Step What it does: Ports and backends. If it fails or is weak: Selector. Policy and routes Security What it does: Allowed and routed. If it fails or is weak: Silent drops. Node and conntrack Step What it does: Rules and tables. If it fails or is weak: Table full. Incident: Random resets under load Incident A busy service had random connection failures that no policy or Service change explained. $ sudo conntrack -S | head -3 cpu=0 found=912 invalid=3 insert=4410 insert_failed=1873 drop=1873 $ sudo sysctl net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max = 131072 net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_count = 131060 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The connection tracking table was full, so new connections were dropped at random. Raising nf_conntrack_max, reducing short lived connections with keepalives and moving DNS to a node local cache fixed the faults. When symptoms are random and load dependent, check node level tables before changing application settings. Common mistakes Skipping the Service and endpoint ch"}});